{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GUvFzrwz3WYScsg9TSSocp","type":"tool_result","content":"{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Evaluating the reliability (marginal, conditional, shift-robust coverage versus set size) of split conformal prediction-set methods applied to compositional softmax outputs on synthetic multi-family benchmarks.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Split conformal prediction with LAC score\",\n    \"Mondrian (class-conditional / confidence-bin) conformal calibration\",\n    \"Gated domain-classifier weighted conformal under covariate/label shift\",\n    \"Group-indicator quantile-regression conformal thresholds with fallback\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"LAC\",\n    \"APS\",\n    \"RAPS\",\n    \"classwise conformal\",\n    \"uncalibrated top-mass baseline\",\n    \"trivial baselines\",\n    \"negative controls (permuted calibration labels, shuffled test labels, constant uniform input)\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"Evaluator-generated synthetic softmax families (K in {10,50,100}: exchangeable, temperature-miscalibrated, long-tailed prior, heteroscedastic, covariate/label shift)\",\n    \"No real dataset (e.g. CIFAR-10) was used in the run\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [\n    \"Plain LAC -> Mondrian grouping -> gated importance-weighted Mondrian -> gated quantile-regression thresholds (no gain over previous variant)\"\n  ],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"Exact marginal validity coexists with residual worst-slice coverage gap\",\n    \"Importance weighting reduces shift coverage gap but increases set size\",\n    \"Quantile-regression conditional thresholds gave no measurable gain over weighted Mondrian in search\",\n    \"Beats best baseline on final evaluation with CI excluding zero\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"least ambiguous set-valued classifiers conformal LAC softmax score\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"LAC (1 - softmax of true class) is the base nonconformity score in all three variants; need its original set-valued-classifier source.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"adaptive prediction sets APS RAPS conformal classification regularization\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Evaluator runs APS and RAPS as baselines on identical synthetic softmax data; final score margin over best baseline (+0.039, CI [0.027,0.050]) is the headline comparison.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Mondrian conformal prediction class-conditional label-conditional validity\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Chosen configurations are Mondrian (class-conditional for K<=10, confidence-bin for K>10); classwise baseline also in evaluator.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"clustered conformal prediction many classes class-conditional coverage\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"K in {10,50,100} with limited per-class calibration data; confidence-bin grouping for K>10 relates to clustering/grouping remedies for class-conditional coverage.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"weighted conformal prediction covariate shift label shift importance weights\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Best configuration adds gated domain-classifier importance weights (test mass at +inf, effective-sample-size margins); shift gap fell from 0.061 to 0.020 in search runs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"density ratio estimation probabilistic classifier importance weighting covariate shift\",\n      \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Weights come from a cross-fitted ridge-logistic domain classifier with a held-out shift-detection gate, an implied density-ratio-by-classification literature.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"conformal prediction conditional coverage impossibility group-conditional guarantees\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Worst-slice coverage gap (class/size/difficulty strata) stays 0.04-0.07 despite exact marginal validity (~0.001-0.0003 gap); must cite limits of distribution-free conditional coverage and group-conditional methods.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"conformal prediction conditional coverage via quantile regression of nonconformity scores\",\n      \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Third variant used group-indicator pinball quantile-regression thresholds with out-of-fold offset and exact fallback; it gave identical scores to the second variant (fallback/no gain), a result needing this literature.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"size-stratified coverage metric evaluating conformal classifiers adaptiveness\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Reliability protocol is multi-axis (marginal gap, worst-slice gap, set-size ratio, shift gap, negative controls); need prior evaluation metrics for set-valued classifiers.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"compositional data analysis simplex log-ratio transformation Aitchison geometry\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Outputs are softmax simplex vectors (Aitchison framing); motivates simplex-valued outputs and what is and is not exploited by scalar-score conformal methods.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"generic 'benchmarking' or 'optimization' phrasing\",\n    \"campaign/search-history or milestone-shaped queries\",\n    \"CIFAR-10 / real-image dataset queries as main topic (not used in run)\",\n    \"regression-only conformal intervals unrelated to set-valued classification\",\n    \"the withheld prior paper on this task, its title/authors/results or any page citing it\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}# Role\nYou are the post-draft reference-planning agent for a completed research run.\nGoal: produce a grounded incremental reference query plan from an existing paper draft.\nUse grounded inputs only. Do not retrieve references yet.\n\n29:\\title{Gated Weighted-Mondrian Conformal Sets for Softmax Outputs: A Multi-Axis Reliability Evaluation on Synthetic Simplex Families}\n36:Softmax outputs are compositions: non-negative vectors summing to one. Conformal prediction turns them into label sets with a finite-sample marginal coverage guarantee, but marginal coverage says little about reliability for particular classes, confidence strata, or shifted data. We study a split-conformal procedure that combines Mondrian (class- or confidence-bin-conditional) calibration of the least-ambiguous-set score with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step that reduces exactly to the unweighted procedure when no shift is detected. We evaluate it under a fixed, evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families: a composite score combining one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio, plus three negative controls. On sealed final data (five seeded runs, no failed episodes, all controls passed) the procedure scores 0.8416 against 0.8029 for the best of four standard split-conformal baselines; the paired margin is 0.0387 (bootstrap 95\\% interval 0.0274--0.0499). Residual under-coverage remains on shifted cases (0.0536) and worst slices (0.0675). The result is conditional on the declared synthetic families; baseline identities, per-case results and real-data behaviour are unavailable.\n40:Many predictors return a composition: a vector of non-negative entries summing to one, such as a classifier's softmax probabilities. Users of such outputs need to know when the associated uncertainty statement can be trusted. Conformal prediction converts a score function and a held-out calibration set into prediction sets whose marginal coverage is at least $1-\\alpha$ under exchangeability, in finite samples and without distributional assumptions. In practice, however, three questions that matter to a user are not answered by the marginal guarantee: does coverage hold within classes or confidence strata, does it survive a change in the input distribution, and is it achieved with sets that are small enough to be informative? Variants that address one of these questions typically do so at the cost of another \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Kiyani2024LengthOI}, so comparisons that report only average coverage and average set size can hide the trade-off.\n42:This paper takes the evaluation protocol as its first-class object. We adopt a single evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families that scores a set-valued predictor on three axes at once (one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio) and that adds three negative controls whose expected behaviour is fixed before scoring. Within this protocol we ask:\n51:(iv) We delimit scope: the evidence concerns synthetic families, one sealed evaluation, and one aggregated baseline comparison. We make no claim of a new theorem or of a compositional-geometry method; the procedure operates on summary features of the probability vector.\n56:\\caption{Evaluation protocol at a glance. Simplex-valued softmax outputs from evaluator-generated synthetic families are split into calibration and test parts. The studied procedure (Mondrian LAC with gated domain-classifier weighting) and four evaluator baselines (LAC, APS, RAPS, class-wise LAC) produce label sets, which are scored on three axes (marginal under-coverage with weight 0.5, worst-slice under-coverage 0.25, set-size ratio 0.25) and checked by three negative controls. Scope is limited to the declared synthetic families.}\n71:Our contribution is neither a new conformal score nor a new guarantee. It is a combined reading of one evaluation protocol and one composition of established components (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, and a held-out gate), with sealed evidence and an explicit account of where it fails. Compositional-data analysis in the sense of log-ratio geometry motivates the framing (outputs live on the simplex), but the procedure studied here does not use log-ratio coordinates for its prediction sets.\n75:Let $p(x)\\in\\Delta^{K-1}$ be the softmax output of a fixed $K$-class predictor, with $y\\in\\{1,\\dots,K\\}$. A conformal procedure receives $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}$ labelled calibration pairs $(p_i,y_i)$, the level $\\alpha$, and $n_{\\mathrm{test}}$ unlabelled test vectors, and returns a set $\\mathcal C(x)\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,K\\}$ for each test vector. The evaluator owns the data: logits are $z=\\epsilon+s\\,d\\,e_y+\\beta\\log\\pi_{\\mathrm{cal}}$ with $\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I_K)$, signal strength $s$, a log-normal heteroscedastic multiplier $d$ on the true-class signal, a prior tilt $\\beta$, and temperature scaling before the softmax; the class prior is balanced or long-tailed. Test data may differ from calibration data through the class prior and a signal-strength multiplier (``shifted'' cases); otherwise a case is exchangeable. The declared range is $2\\le K\\le 100$, $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\le 2000$, $n_{\\mathrm{test}}\\le 4000$ in the search-time evaluator source; the sealed final case manifest (families, $K$, $\\alpha$, sample sizes) is not retained and is therefore unknown to us.\n205:(vi) \\emph{Range.} The tested range is limited by the evaluator ($K\\le100$, $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\le2000$); nothing is claimed about larger $K$, very small calibration sets, or simplex-valued regression targets.\n227:The synthetic generator is one parametric family (Gaussian logits with a true-class signal, heteroscedastic signal strength, a prior tilt and temperature), and the predictor's calibration is not varied independently; real softmax outputs may behave differently. For $K\\le10$ the evaluator's slices are classes, which are exactly the Mondrian groups of the studied procedure, so part of its advantage on the slice axis reflects a match between method and metric by design; the class-wise baseline shares this alignment, but its individual score is not retained. The development score was used to select the configuration, so development numbers are optimistic selection-biased proxies; the sealed evaluation was run once on disjoint data. Finally, the composite loss multiplies the marginal gap by $1/\\alpha$ and clips it, so under-coverage beyond $\\alpha$ in a single case is not distinguished; the retained aggregates cannot show whether any case hit this clip.\n231:\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For users, the results suggest evaluating conformal sets on compositional outputs with at least a marginal, a slice-level and a size criterion, and with controls that expose vacuous behaviour; reporting only average coverage and size would not have revealed the residual shift and slice gaps seen here. A stronger study built on the same protocol would additionally report per-baseline and per-case scores, a within-case ablation that separates the Mondrian grouping, the gate and the weighting, activation logs for the gate, and at least one evaluation on classifier outputs from a natural dataset. We also recommend reporting the three components separately for every method (Figure~\\ref{fig:decomp}).\n234:We studied a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step under a multi-axis reliability protocol on synthetic softmax families. On sealed final data it reached a composite score of 0.8416 versus 0.8029 for the best of four standard baselines, a paired margin of 0.0387 with a bootstrap 95\\% interval of 0.0274--0.0499, with no failed episodes and all three negative controls passed. The same evaluation shows that the procedure does not remove under-coverage under shift (0.0536) or on worst slices (0.0675), and the loss decomposition identifies marginal under-coverage as the largest residual contributor. The conclusions are conditional on the declared synthetic families, on one aggregated best-baseline comparison, and on evaluator-defined weights. The most useful next steps are per-case and per-baseline reporting, ablations that isolate the gate and the weighting from the Mondrian grouping, gate-activation logging, and an evaluation on real classifier outputs; none of these has been done here.\n342:The figures and derived numbers are produced by \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} and \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_stats.py} from the evaluator's recorded outputs; the candidate sources and the evaluator source are included in the artifact bundle, and the evaluator requires NumPy 2.2.6 (SciPy is optional in the candidate). Development seeds are 101--103 and final seeds 9001--9005; the final runs took 4--8 seconds each. The sealed final case manifest and the per-baseline results are not retained, so the final numbers cannot be regenerated from the retained files and must be read as evaluator-reported values. No external dataset was used (so no data licence applies), and no real-data experiment was run, including the CIFAR-10 option offered by the task. Bibliographic descriptions in Section~\\ref{sec:related} rely on titles and venues in the retrieval metadata only; several standard references on split-conformal theory, covariate-shift weighting and compositional data analysis are not in the retrieved bibliography and are therefore stated as background without citation.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a9c6cd30-b13b-4fc9-872d-c09885072b37","uuid":"f0196438-2da3-467d-8b78-0208198adf3b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:02:57.857Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Evaluating the reliability (marginal, conditional, shift-robust coverage versus set size) of split conformal prediction-set methods applied to compositional softmax outputs on synthetic multi-family benchmarks.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Split conformal prediction w